# Suppressions for the `Dependency audit` CI gate (.github/workflows/ci.yml).
#
# Trivy auto-detects THIS filename in the scan working directory (`--ignorefile`
# defaults to `.trivyignore`), so the gate honours this file with no workflow
# edit. Do NOT rename it to `.trivyignore.yaml`: that filename is a valid Trivy
# format but is NOT auto-detected, so it silently suppresses nothing — a
# suppression that looks handled and isn't. Measured on trivy 0.70.0, the
# version pinned by trivy-action@v0.36.0, against this very lockfile:
#
#   .trivyignore.yaml, auto-detected                  -> exit 1 (NOT suppressed)
#   .trivyignore.yaml, explicit --ignorefile           -> exit 0 (suppressed)
#   .trivyignore with `exp:` in the future             -> exit 0 (suppressed)
#   .trivyignore with `exp:` in the past               -> exit 1 (lapsed)
#   .trivyignore with a malformed `exp:`               -> exit 1 (fails closed)
#
# FORMAT, enforced by scripts/ci/trivyignore-policy.test.mjs:
#   * one suppression per line, `<ID> exp:YYYY-MM-DD` — the `exp:` is mandatory.
#     Trivy stops honouring the line once the date passes, so the exception has
#     to be re-decided instead of calcifying. A malformed date suppresses
#     nothing, which is why the test insists on the exact shape.
#   * the comment block directly above each line must carry a `statement:`
#     explaining why the vulnerable code is UNREACHABLE for us, not merely that
#     the finding is inconvenient.
#   * never raise `severity` or flip `exit-code` to 0 in ci.yml instead of
#     adding a line here (ci.yml's gate comment forbids it explicitly).

# ── CVE-2026-93687 · braces@3.0.3 · HIGH ────────────────────────────────────
# DoS via stack overflow from deeply nested brace patterns.
#
# Published between 15:36Z (grove-sites' last green `CI` run, 37334204779) and
# 19:40Z on 2026-10-05, at which point `Dependency audit` went red on EVERY open
# grove-sites PR. Because auto-approve.yml requires the whole `CI` run green
# before it merges, that wedged the repo's entire agent merge path, not just one
# PR. Trivy reports no fixed version — the `Fixed Version` column is empty and
# 3.0.3 is the current braces release — so there is nothing to upgrade or
# `pnpm override` to.
#
# Dependency path — dev-only, lint-time, exactly one chain:
#   @next/eslint-plugin-next@16.2.1 (devDependency)
#     -> fast-glob@3.3.1 -> micromatch@4.0.8 -> braces@3.0.3
# Verified against pnpm-lock.yaml: the only dependent of braces is micromatch,
# whose only dependent is fast-glob, whose only dependent is the ESLint plugin.
# No workspace package.json declares braces or micromatch directly.
#
# statement: Dev-only transitive dependency reached solely through
#   @next/eslint-plugin-next -> fast-glob -> micromatch -> braces. The brace
#   patterns braces expands are built by the ESLint plugin from our own eslint
#   configuration and repo-local file paths, so no untrusted input ever reaches
#   one; and the package is a devDependency present in no runtime image and no
#   deployed bundle, so there is no production attack surface at all. The impact
#   ceiling is "a lint job crashes on a pattern we wrote ourselves". No fixed
#   version is published yet; upgrade out of this suppression as soon as one is.
CVE-2026-93687 exp:2026-11-04
